=== 1. no X11 socket, auto -> VNC with a hint
(Termux:X11 not detected - for the faster display, run 'spft' in Termux)
spft-gui called
box64 prints a few 'Cannot dlopen' / 'Error loading needed lib' lines at the start. They are normal.
Only a red BOX64 in front of 'Error:' is a real problem.
starting SP Flash Tool from $T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test (Ctrl+C to stop)
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/plugins
BOX64 libpath=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/lib:$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test:/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu:/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
BOX64 preload=unset
exit=0

=== 2. X11 socket present, auto -> Termux:X11
display: Termux:X11
box64 prints a few 'Cannot dlopen' / 'Error loading needed lib' lines at the start. They are normal.
Only a red BOX64 in front of 'Error:' is a real problem.
starting SP Flash Tool from $T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test (Ctrl+C to stop)
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:0 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/plugins
BOX64 libpath=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/lib:$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test:/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu:/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
BOX64 preload=unset
exit=0
openbox starts so far: 1

=== 3. run again -> openbox must not be started twice
openbox starts so far: 1

=== 4. forced vnc while socket exists -> VNC
spft-gui called
box64 prints a few 'Cannot dlopen' / 'Error loading needed lib' lines at the start. They are normal.
Only a red BOX64 in front of 'Error:' is a real problem.
starting SP Flash Tool from $T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test (Ctrl+C to stop)
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/plugins
BOX64 libpath=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/lib:$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test:/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu:/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu
BOX64 preload=unset
exit=0

=== 5. forced x11 without socket -> clear message, exit 1
Termux:X11 is not running, or Debian was entered before it started.
In Termux run:  spft
exit=1

=== 6. bad mode -> message, exit 1
SPFT_DISPLAY must be x11 or vnc (or unset)
exit=1

=== 7. no tool installed -> tells you to run: spft tool, exit 1
SP Flash Tool is not installed yet. In Termux run:  spft tool
exit=1

=== 8. a copy is already running -> no second copy, exit 0
SP Flash Tool is already running (pid N), so no second copy is started.
Switch to the Termux:X11 app (or your VNC viewer) to see it.
To close it first, run in Termux:  spft stop   (never while a flash is running)
exit=0
--- once it is gone, the tool starts normally again:
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/plugins
BOX64 libpath=$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test/lib:$T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test:/usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu:/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu

=== 9. same, when only the process NAME is flash_tool (the command line was rewritten)
SP Flash Tool is already running (pid N), so no second copy is started.
exit=0

=== 10. Termux:X11, first start: rc.xml is made from Debian's default, plus one maximize rule
display: Termux:X11
starting SP Flash Tool from $T/opt/SP_Flash_Tool_test (Ctrl+C to stop)
rc.xml is well-formed XML; rules: 1
  class=* type=normal maximized=yes
everything else in rc.xml is identical to Debian's default
openbox starts: 1   reconfigures: 0

=== 11. second start keeps an edited rc.xml and does not start openbox again
rc.xml untouched
openbox starts: 1   reconfigures: 0

=== 12. rc.xml made while openbox already runs -> openbox is told to reload it
openbox starts: 1   reconfigures: 1
rc.xml written again

=== 13. Debian has no default rc.xml -> nothing is written, the tool still starts
1
ok: no rc.xml written

=== 14. /opt/spft-current decides which version starts
starting SP Flash Tool from $T/opt/spft-current (Ctrl+C to stop)
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=unset
--- and without the link, one of them is found:
1

=== 15. the v6 program name (SPFlashToolV6) is found and started
BOX64 args=[./SPFlashToolV6] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=unset

=== 16. a running SPFlashToolV6 also blocks a second copy
SP Flash Tool is already running (pid N), so no second copy is started.
exit=0

=== 17. kernel 4.19.127: SP Flash Tool v5 wrongly rejects it (a text comparison), so it is shown 4.19.140
kernel 4.19.127-g8d3429bdb336: SP Flash Tool v5 refuses kernels like this one by mistake (it seems to compare versions as text), so it is shown 4.19.140.
(to turn this off: SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL=off spft)
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=unset
BOX64 preload=$T/kernelfix.so
the file the library reads says: 4.19.140
ok: the library was decoded from lib/kernelfix.so.b64

=== 18. a kernel the tool accepts: nothing is shown, no library is loaded, and the file of the last run is gone
BOX64 preload=unset
ok: no fake-kernel file

=== 19. an old kernel (4.14.186): left alone; SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL=5.4.0 forces a version
BOX64 preload=unset
kernel 4.14.186: SP Flash Tool is shown the kernel version 5.4.0, as SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL says.
BOX64 preload=$T/kernelfix.so
the file says: 5.4.0

=== 20. SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL=off turns the fix off, also for 4.19.127
BOX64 preload=unset
ok: no fake-kernel file

=== 21. a bad SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL is refused with a message, and the tool still starts
SPFT_FAKE_KERNEL must be auto, off, or a version like 5.4.0 - ignoring '5.4;touch /tmp/pwned'
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=unset
BOX64 preload=unset
ok: nothing was run

=== 22. a BOX64_LD_PRELOAD that is already set stays, the fix comes first
BOX64 preload=$T/kernelfix.so:/opt/other.so

=== 23. the kernel-fix files are not there (an old Debian setup): the tool simply starts
BOX64 args=[./flash_tool] DISPLAY=:1 NOMITSHM=1 QTPLUG=unset
BOX64 preload=unset

=== 24. the tool starts with the phone's internal storage as its home folder, so that its file windows open there
file windows: start in $T/storage (the phone's internal storage). To turn this off: SPFT_STORAGE=off spft
BOX64 home=$T/storage xdgconf=$T/home/.config xdgcache=$T/home/.cache xdgdata=$T/home/.local/share
--- the tool's own settings and caches stay where they were (Debian's /root, here $T/home): the XDG folders above say so

=== 25. SPFT_STORAGE=auto is /sdcard (a stand-in folder here); when that is not there, home stays as it is
file windows: start in $T/sdcard (the phone's internal storage). To turn this off: SPFT_STORAGE=off spft
BOX64 home=$T/sdcard xdgconf=$T/home/.config xdgcache=$T/home/.cache xdgdata=$T/home/.local/share
--- not there:
(the folder $T/sdcard is not there, so the file windows start in $T/home)
BOX64 home=$T/home xdgconf=unset xdgcache=unset xdgdata=unset
--- a folder that was asked for by name and is not there:
(the folder $T/no-such-folder is not there, so the file windows start in $T/home)
BOX64 home=$T/home xdgconf=unset xdgcache=unset xdgdata=unset

=== 26. SPFT_STORAGE=off: home stays, and no XDG folder is set
BOX64 home=$T/home xdgconf=unset xdgcache=unset xdgdata=unset

=== 27. an XDG folder that is set already is kept as it is
BOX64 home=$T/storage xdgconf=/my/conf xdgcache=$T/home/.cache xdgdata=$T/home/.local/share

=== 28. only the tool gets the new home: openbox's rc.xml is still made in Debian's own home, and nothing is written to the storage
BOX64 home=$T/storage xdgconf=$T/home/.config xdgcache=$T/home/.cache xdgdata=$T/home/.local/share
ok: rc.xml is in Debian's home
ok: the storage folder is untouched
cleaned up
